# HUMAN co-authors accepted in this repository. An ALLOWLIST, not a blocklist.
#
# WHY IT IS INVERTED. The natural way to write this rule is to enumerate the AI
# assistants whose trailers should be refused. That list is stale the week after
# it is written — a new assistant ships, writes a `Co-authored-by` trailer git
# recognises like any other, and the rule passes it without a word. The humans
# working on a project are a short list that changes about once a year.
#
# So the rule is: a co-authorship trailer passes if, and only if, its address is
# on this list. An AI does not sign commits here. If you want the trailer never
# to be generated in the first place — which is the real fix, because then there
# is no false positive to argue about — in Claude Code that is
#   .claude/settings.json  ->  { "includeCoAuthoredBy": false }
#
# FORMAT: one identity per line, either "Name <address>" or a bare address. What
# is compared is the ADDRESS, lower-cased; the display name is free text and
# identifies nobody. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# READ BY: .githooks/check-message.mjs, from the commit-msg hook, before the
# commit exists.
#
# KEEP THIS FILE TRACKED. If it is missing, the hook fails closed and refuses
# every co-authorship trailer — deleting a file must never be the easy way to
# switch a rule off.

